1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,198
You wake up. The ground is warm.

2
00:00:02,198 --> 00:00:04,570
And something is breathing on your face.

3
00:00:04,871 --> 00:00:06,450
YOU: Okay. Okay. Nobody move.

4
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:08,028
YOU: Especially you, big guy.

5
00:00:08,329 --> 00:00:10,627
TROOD: Don't worry. He only eats things that run.

6
00:00:10,627 --> 00:00:11,830
TROOD: So, please, don't run.

7
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:14,129
Sixty-six million years ago,

8
00:00:14,129 --> 00:00:16,842
a giant asteroid was heading for Earth.

9
00:00:16,842 --> 00:00:18,581
In this world, it missed.

10
00:00:18,581 --> 00:00:20,042
And now, you're here.

11
00:00:22,242 --> 00:00:23,553
YOU: Wait. You can talk?

12
00:00:23,753 --> 00:00:24,956
TROOD: Of course I can talk.

13
00:00:24,956 --> 00:00:27,878
TROOD: I'm a troodont. Seventy million years of evolution.

14
00:00:27,878 --> 00:00:29,768
TROOD: You're the one who looks strange.

15
00:00:30,068 --> 00:00:31,101
YOU: Where I come from,

16
00:00:31,101 --> 00:00:33,451
YOU: a rock ten kilometers wide hit Mexico and

17
00:00:33,451 --> 00:00:34,828
YOU: wiped out the dinosaurs.

18
00:00:35,078 --> 00:00:36,984
TROOD: Ten kilometers? That's a mountain.

19
00:00:36,984 --> 00:00:37,770
TROOD: What happened?

20
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:40,723
The impact threw so much soot and dust

21
00:00:40,723 --> 00:00:43,212
into the sky that the sun went dark

22
00:00:43,212 --> 00:00:46,556
for months. About three quarters of all species

23
00:00:46,556 --> 00:00:50,326
on Earth died, including every dinosaur that couldn't

24
00:00:50,326 --> 00:00:50,610
fly.

25
00:00:50,911 --> 00:00:52,375
YOU: And here's the crazy part.

26
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:54,629
YOU: It hit one of the worst possible places,

27
00:00:54,629 --> 00:00:56,263
YOU: rocks full of oil and sulfur.

28
00:00:56,263 --> 00:00:59,081
YOU: Scientists calculated that if it had landed almost

29
00:00:59,081 --> 00:01:01,842
YOU: anywhere else, the dinosaurs might have survived.

30
00:01:02,142 --> 00:01:03,829
TROOD: So we were just unlucky?

31
00:01:04,079 --> 00:01:06,648
YOU: Very unlucky. A study from twenty seventeen says

32
00:01:06,648 --> 00:01:09,163
YOU: only about thirteen percent of the planet would

33
00:01:09,163 --> 00:01:10,769
YOU: have caused a mass extinction.

34
00:01:11,069 --> 00:01:13,880
YOU: Some scientists back home even say dinosaurs were

35
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:16,117
YOU: already in decline before the asteroid.

36
00:01:16,367 --> 00:01:18,540
TROOD: Rude. Do I look like I'm in decline?

37
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:20,704
That idea is still debated.

38
00:01:20,704 --> 00:01:23,742
Many dinosaur groups seem to have been doing

39
00:01:23,742 --> 00:01:25,814
fine right up to the very end.

40
00:01:28,013 --> 00:01:29,667
Your guide's name is Trood.

41
00:01:29,667 --> 00:01:32,055
She leads you through a forest that has

42
00:01:32,055 --> 00:01:33,280
never seen a winter.

43
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:35,396
YOU: It's so hot. Where is all the ice?

44
00:01:35,646 --> 00:01:36,671
TROOD: Ice? On the poles,

45
00:01:36,671 --> 00:01:37,982
TROOD: there are only forests.

46
00:01:37,982 --> 00:01:40,147
TROOD: And the sea comes much further inland.

47
00:01:40,447 --> 00:01:42,176
In the age of dinosaurs,

48
00:01:42,176 --> 00:01:44,120
the Earth was a greenhouse.

49
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:46,784
No ice caps. Sea levels were around a

50
00:01:46,784 --> 00:01:49,160
hundred meters higher than today.

51
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:52,256
In this world, the planet never cooled down

52
00:01:52,256 --> 00:01:52,761
so far.

53
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:55,714
YOU: Are those flowers? I thought dinosaurs lived with

54
00:01:55,714 --> 00:01:56,039
YOU: ferns.

55
00:01:56,289 --> 00:01:57,991
TROOD: Ferns are for my grandparents.

56
00:01:57,991 --> 00:01:59,296
TROOD: We have flowers, fruit,

57
00:01:59,296 --> 00:02:02,020
TROOD: and grass. They appeared while my ancestors were

58
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:02,757
TROOD: still around.

59
00:02:03,058 --> 00:02:04,408
YOU: And something just bit me.

60
00:02:04,408 --> 00:02:05,965
YOU: Are there mosquitoes here too?

61
00:02:06,215 --> 00:02:08,423
TROOD: Of course. Insects are older than all of

62
00:02:08,423 --> 00:02:10,299
TROOD: us. And your blood is new to them.

63
00:02:10,299 --> 00:02:11,347
TROOD: They will love you.

64
00:02:13,548 --> 00:02:14,148
YOU: Is that a T.

65
00:02:14,148 --> 00:02:14,899
YOU: rex? I knew it!

66
00:02:14,899 --> 00:02:16,352
YOU: I always knew they were real!

67
00:02:16,602 --> 00:02:18,303
TROOD: A what? That's a tyrannosaur,

68
00:02:18,303 --> 00:02:20,297
TROOD: yes, but the one you know died out

69
00:02:20,297 --> 00:02:21,940
TROOD: sixty-six million years ago,

70
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:24,169
TROOD: even here. Species don't last forever.

71
00:02:24,169 --> 00:02:25,929
TROOD: This is its great-great-great,

72
00:02:25,929 --> 00:02:27,219
TROOD: a million times great,

73
00:02:27,219 --> 00:02:27,865
TROOD: grandchild.

74
00:02:28,164 --> 00:02:29,220
YOU: Does it have feathers?

75
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:31,428
YOU: Back home, scientists argue about that all the

76
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:31,667
YOU: time.

77
00:02:31,918 --> 00:02:33,459
YOU: And how hard can it bite?

78
00:02:33,658 --> 00:02:35,382
TROOD: Hard enough to crush a car.

79
00:02:35,382 --> 00:02:36,532
TROOD: Whatever a car is.

80
00:02:36,831 --> 00:02:38,716
TROOD: Feathers? Babies do, for warmth.

81
00:02:38,716 --> 00:02:40,541
TROOD: The big ones are mostly scales.

82
00:02:40,541 --> 00:02:42,367
TROOD: Too hot for a fur coat when you

83
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:44,074
TROOD: weigh as much as an elephant.

84
00:02:44,375 --> 00:02:48,026
Skin fossils of big tyrannosaurs show mostly scales.

85
00:02:48,026 --> 00:02:50,625
Their smaller relatives had feathers.

86
00:02:50,875 --> 00:02:52,185
YOU: What is that, in the sky?

87
00:02:52,185 --> 00:02:53,024
YOU: Is that a plane?

88
00:02:53,223 --> 00:02:54,821
TROOD: A pterosaur. When it lands,

89
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:56,360
TROOD: it's as tall as a giraffe.

90
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:58,194
TROOD: Its wings are wider than a bus.

91
00:02:58,495 --> 00:02:59,899
YOU: And the ground is shaking.

92
00:02:59,899 --> 00:03:01,302
YOU: Why is the ground shaking?

93
00:03:01,502 --> 00:03:03,426
TROOD: Long-necks. Two hundred of them.

94
00:03:03,426 --> 00:03:04,688
TROOD: Step behind the tree,

95
00:03:04,688 --> 00:03:05,109
TROOD: please.

96
00:03:07,308 --> 00:03:09,270
Trood takes you to the coast.

97
00:03:09,270 --> 00:03:10,352
The sea is warm,

98
00:03:10,352 --> 00:03:13,124
shallow, and full of things you have only

99
00:03:13,124 --> 00:03:14,207
seen in museums.

100
00:03:14,456 --> 00:03:15,588
YOU: Can I swim?

101
00:03:15,788 --> 00:03:17,076
TROOD: You can. Once.

102
00:03:17,377 --> 00:03:20,148
Something huge rises out of the water.

103
00:03:20,148 --> 00:03:22,920
A mosasaur: a sea lizard longer than a

104
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:25,692
school bus, with a double row of teeth

105
00:03:25,692 --> 00:03:27,516
in the roof of its mouth.

106
00:03:27,765 --> 00:03:29,267
YOU: Okay, I'll stay on the sand.

107
00:03:29,267 --> 00:03:30,394
YOU: Are there sharks too?

108
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:32,611
TROOD: Sharks are older than both of us.

109
00:03:32,611 --> 00:03:34,102
TROOD: They survived everything.

110
00:03:34,401 --> 00:03:36,543
TROOD: Smart. Your species learns fast.

111
00:03:36,793 --> 00:03:39,685
Mosasaurs died out with the dinosaurs.

112
00:03:39,685 --> 00:03:41,741
Here, they rule the oceans.

113
00:03:41,741 --> 00:03:43,264
There are no whales,

114
00:03:43,264 --> 00:03:44,938
no dolphins, no seals.

115
00:03:44,938 --> 00:03:48,136
Those were all mammals that grew big after

116
00:03:48,136 --> 00:03:49,126
the asteroid.

117
00:03:51,325 --> 00:03:52,975
YOU: Wait. If dinosaurs are still here,

118
00:03:52,975 --> 00:03:54,043
YOU: where are the mammals?

119
00:03:54,043 --> 00:03:55,061
YOU: Where are the people?

120
00:03:55,312 --> 00:03:57,306
PIP: Down here! Hi! Please don't step on me!

121
00:03:57,556 --> 00:03:58,334
YOU: A mouse?

122
00:03:58,533 --> 00:03:59,881
PIP: A primate! Well, almost.

123
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:01,396
PIP: I eat fruit, I climb trees,

124
00:04:01,396 --> 00:04:02,855
PIP: and I'm the size of a rat.

125
00:04:02,855 --> 00:04:05,212
PIP: My family has been doing this for millions

126
00:04:05,212 --> 00:04:05,717
PIP: of years.

127
00:04:06,018 --> 00:04:10,066
Real fossils of a tiny tree-climber called Purgatorius

128
00:04:10,066 --> 00:04:12,465
date to just after the asteroid.

129
00:04:12,465 --> 00:04:16,589
Its family probably lived alongside the last dinosaurs.

130
00:04:16,589 --> 00:04:19,512
It may be one of your oldest ancestors.

131
00:04:19,812 --> 00:04:21,502
YOU: So you're my great-grandparent?

132
00:04:21,702 --> 00:04:24,370
PIP: Your great-great-great, a few million times great!

133
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:25,810
PIP: But here, we never grew up.

134
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:27,357
PIP: Every time a mammal gets big,

135
00:04:27,357 --> 00:04:28,904
PIP: something with teeth eats it.

136
00:04:29,204 --> 00:04:30,720
That's the hard truth.

137
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,856
With dinosaurs ruling the land,

138
00:04:32,856 --> 00:04:35,680
mammals would probably have stayed small.

139
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:36,989
No apes. No humans.

140
00:04:36,989 --> 00:04:39,401
In this world, you shouldn't exist.

141
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:42,831
YOU: Then who built that?

142
00:04:43,081 --> 00:04:44,502
TROOD: Our nests? My family did.

143
00:04:44,502 --> 00:04:46,035
TROOD: Bigger brain, better hands,

144
00:04:46,035 --> 00:04:47,625
TROOD: and we've had a lot of time.

145
00:04:47,925 --> 00:04:52,637
In nineteen eighty-two, the paleontologist Dale Russell imagined

146
00:04:52,637 --> 00:04:55,656
this exact idea. He took a smart dinosaur

147
00:04:55,656 --> 00:04:57,497
called Troodon and asked:

148
00:04:57,497 --> 00:04:59,337
what if it kept evolving?

149
00:04:59,337 --> 00:05:02,061
He called the result the dinosauroid.

150
00:05:02,362 --> 00:05:04,267
YOU: But yours doesn't look human at all.

151
00:05:04,517 --> 00:05:06,435
TROOD: Why would I? Your body is good for

152
00:05:06,435 --> 00:05:08,464
TROOD: your history. Mine is good for mine.

153
00:05:08,464 --> 00:05:09,367
TROOD: I keep the tail.

154
00:05:09,367 --> 00:05:10,663
TROOD: It's great for balance.

155
00:05:10,963 --> 00:05:15,155
Many scientists criticized Russell's human-shaped version.

156
00:05:15,155 --> 00:05:18,335
A smart dinosaur would more likely look like

157
00:05:18,335 --> 00:05:19,709
a big, clever bird.

158
00:05:20,009 --> 00:05:21,852
TROOD: So tell me. What did your kind do

159
00:05:21,852 --> 00:05:23,024
TROOD: with your big brains?

160
00:05:23,274 --> 00:05:25,290
YOU: We built cities. We flew to the Moon.

161
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:27,305
YOU: We invented pizza. And we also made a

162
00:05:27,305 --> 00:05:28,176
YOU: pretty big mess.

163
00:05:28,476 --> 00:05:31,129
TROOD: Hmm. Maybe that asteroid did your planet a

164
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:32,391
TROOD: favor. Or maybe not.

165
00:05:32,692 --> 00:05:34,423
Trood shows you her town:

166
00:05:34,423 --> 00:05:36,987
hundreds of woven nests in the trees,

167
00:05:36,987 --> 00:05:40,173
and a field where her family herds duck-billed

168
00:05:40,173 --> 00:05:41,697
dinosaurs like cattle.

169
00:05:41,947 --> 00:05:43,504
YOU: You farm dinosaurs?

170
00:05:43,704 --> 00:05:45,178
TROOD: You farm birds, don't you?

171
00:05:45,178 --> 00:05:47,503
TROOD: Same thing. These ones give eggs the size

172
00:05:47,503 --> 00:05:48,240
TROOD: of your head.

173
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:50,853
YOU: Okay, that's actually breakfast for a week.

174
00:05:53,052 --> 00:05:55,711
YOU: Okay, honest question. Could I survive here?

175
00:05:55,961 --> 00:05:56,771
TROOD: For a few days.

176
00:05:56,771 --> 00:05:58,498
TROOD: Maybe. You're slow, you're soft,

177
00:05:58,498 --> 00:05:59,793
TROOD: and you smell delicious.

178
00:06:00,093 --> 00:06:01,639
YOU: Thank you. Very helpful.

179
00:06:01,838 --> 00:06:03,203
TROOD: The air is fine for you,

180
00:06:03,203 --> 00:06:04,226
TROOD: the fruit is fine.

181
00:06:04,226 --> 00:06:06,556
TROOD: But at night, the small hunters come out,

182
00:06:06,556 --> 00:06:07,920
TROOD: and they hunt in groups.

183
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:09,595
PIP: That's why we sleep in holes!

184
00:06:09,595 --> 00:06:10,675
PIP: Want to share my hole?

185
00:06:10,925 --> 00:06:12,255
YOU: I don't think I'll fit.

186
00:06:12,555 --> 00:06:15,560
Night falls. Shapes move between the trees.

187
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:18,286
Small, fast, feathered, and very quiet.

188
00:06:18,536 --> 00:06:20,476
TROOD: Don't move. Raptors see movement.

189
00:06:20,476 --> 00:06:21,358
TROOD: Breathe slowly.

190
00:06:21,608 --> 00:06:22,963
PIP: Breathe slowly, he says.

191
00:06:22,963 --> 00:06:24,882
PIP: My heart beats ten times a second!

192
00:06:25,132 --> 00:06:26,403
YOU: Trood, what do we do?

193
00:06:26,603 --> 00:06:28,028
TROOD: Now? Now we run.

194
00:06:28,329 --> 00:06:30,109
You run. Trood runs faster.

195
00:06:30,109 --> 00:06:31,890
Pip rides on your shoulder,

196
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:33,473
screaming the whole way.

197
00:06:33,723 --> 00:06:34,834
PIP: Left! No, right! No,

198
00:06:34,834 --> 00:06:35,723
PIP: up! Up the tree!

199
00:06:35,973 --> 00:06:37,571
YOU: I can't climb that fast!

200
00:06:37,771 --> 00:06:39,401
TROOD: Then grab my tail!

201
00:06:41,601 --> 00:06:43,414
And then you wake up again.

202
00:06:43,414 --> 00:06:45,159
A real bed. A real window.

203
00:06:45,159 --> 00:06:45,898
Your world.

204
00:06:46,148 --> 00:06:47,310
CROW: Caw. Morning.

205
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:49,158
YOU: Wait. Did you just say morning?

206
00:06:49,408 --> 00:06:50,609
SISTER: It's a crow, not a parrot.

207
00:06:50,609 --> 00:06:52,225
SISTER: And you were talking in your sleep.

208
00:06:52,225 --> 00:06:53,749
SISTER: Something about please don't run?

209
00:06:53,999 --> 00:06:55,613
YOU: Long night. Very long night.

210
00:06:55,913 --> 00:06:58,356
SISTER: Crows can't talk, but they do something just

211
00:06:58,356 --> 00:07:00,077
SISTER: as impressive. They make tools,

212
00:07:00,077 --> 00:07:01,799
SISTER: remember faces, and plan ahead.

213
00:07:01,799 --> 00:07:03,076
SISTER: And they are dinosaurs.

214
00:07:03,375 --> 00:07:04,867
YOU: Dinosaurs? Crows?

215
00:07:05,067 --> 00:07:06,323
SISTER: The asteroid really hit,

216
00:07:06,323 --> 00:07:08,259
SISTER: by the way. The crater is still under

217
00:07:08,259 --> 00:07:09,829
SISTER: Mexico. It's called Chicxulub,

218
00:07:09,829 --> 00:07:12,237
SISTER: and it's about a hundred and eighty kilometers

219
00:07:12,237 --> 00:07:12,498
SISTER: wide.

220
00:07:12,749 --> 00:07:13,689
YOU: So in my world,

221
00:07:13,689 --> 00:07:14,879
YOU: the dinosaurs lost.

222
00:07:15,129 --> 00:07:16,040
SISTER: Not all of them.

223
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:18,943
SISTER: Birds are the dinosaurs that survived the asteroid.

224
00:07:18,943 --> 00:07:20,423
SISTER: In two thousand and eight,

225
00:07:20,423 --> 00:07:22,586
SISTER: scientists compared proteins from a T.

226
00:07:22,586 --> 00:07:25,546
SISTER: rex bone. Its closest living relatives were chickens

227
00:07:25,546 --> 00:07:26,343
SISTER: and ostriches.

228
00:07:26,642 --> 00:07:29,912
There are about eleven thousand species of birds

229
00:07:29,912 --> 00:07:32,908
alive today. And a parrot's brain packs more

230
00:07:32,908 --> 00:07:35,701
neurons than a monkey's brain of the same

231
00:07:35,701 --> 00:07:36,041
size.

232
00:07:36,341 --> 00:07:38,790
YOU: So the dinosaurs never really went extinct.

233
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:40,649
SISTER: Exactly. They just got smaller,

234
00:07:40,649 --> 00:07:42,309
SISTER: grew wings, and learned to sing.

235
00:07:42,658 --> 00:07:44,225
YOU: Morning, little dinosaur.

236
00:07:44,425 --> 00:07:45,031
CROW: Caw!

237
00:07:45,382 --> 00:07:47,862
So, which world would you rather live in?

238
00:07:47,862 --> 00:07:50,343
Theirs, or ours? Tell me in the comments.
